so still about 0.047 left. Continuing\n\nI will stop checking and give the final answer now.\n</think>\n\nWe are given:\n\n$$\np = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^2}, \\quad q = \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{k^3}\n$$\n\nand asked to express the double sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3}\n$$\n\nin terms of $p$ and $q$.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Change of Variables\n\nLet\u2019s define $n = j + k$. Since $j, k \\geq 1$, the smallest possible value of $n = j + k$ is $2$, and $n$ can go to infinity.\n\nFor each fixed $n \\geq 2$, the number of positive integer pairs $(j, k)$ such that $j + k = n$ is $n - 1$. This is because $j$ can range from $1$ to $n - 1$, and $k = n - j$ is uniquely determined.\n\nThus, the double sum becomes:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{j = 1}^\\infty \\sum_{k = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{(j + k)^3} = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\sum_{\\substack{j + k = n \\\\ j, k \\geq 1}} \\frac{1}{n^3} = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Split the Sum\n\nNow, we split the sum:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{n - 1}{n^3} = \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\left( \\frac{1}{n^2} - \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right)\n$$\n\nSo, this becomes:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} \\right) - \\left( \\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} \\right)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Express in Terms of $p$ and $q$\n\nWe know:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p, \\quad \\sum_{n = 1}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^2} = p - \\frac{1}{1^2} = p - 1\n$$\n$$\n\\sum_{n = 2}^\\infty \\frac{1}{n^3} = q - \\frac{1}{1^3} = q - 1\n$$\n\nSo, the original double sum becomes:\n\n$$\n(p - 1) - (q - 1) = p - q\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{p - q}\n$$", "close_id": 151668, "open_id": 151667, "eos_id": 151645, "cue_ids": [271, 40, 686, 2936, 13295, 323, 2968, 279, 1590, 4226, 1431, 624, 151668, 271]}